{
  "requirement": "Every {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion` type implements\n`trust_but_verify::graph::Criterion<D>` for its\n{term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain`.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    type Ok: Serialize + DeserializeOwned + Send;\n\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> D::NodeId;\n    fn criterion_path(&self) -> CriterionPath;\n    fn version(&self) -> CriterionVersion;\n    fn execute(\n        self,\n        ctx: &mut ValidationContext<'_, D>,\n    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Self::Ok, ValidationError<D::Error>>> + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`. This is a type-level requirement. Each criterion type must implement `Criterion<D>`, with `Send + Sized`, an `Ok` that is serializable, deserializable and `Send`, the four accessor and version methods, and an `execute` that returns a `Send` future. Compilation is the main check for that shape. The tests cover each part.\n\n- **Real implementations.** Several tests use `Review` types that implement `Criterion<D>` and run them through `validation::verify`, `store_evidence`, `load_evidence` and `check_inputs`.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` covers a typed `Ok`. It checks the `Ok` round trip, `source_node_id`, `criterion_path` and `version`, and it checks that `execute` runs without writing anything.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[caller_reuses_negative_answers_and_compares_revisions_explicitly]` covers `version()` and `is_compatible_with` on the trait's `Review`.\n  - `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[deterministic_validation_uses_required_storage_without_an_agent]` runs a deterministic criterion that reads the node through `ValidationContext`.\n- **`Send` bounds.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[validation_and_answer_need_send_without_sync]` wraps `verify`, `store_evidence`, `load_evidence` and `refresh_evidence` in a `send(...)` helper. It uses a `Cell` answer type, which is `Send` but not `Sync`. This checks that the `execute` future is `Send`, that `Ok` needs `Send` but not `Sync`, and that the criterion type is `Send`.\n- **A criterion outside the test crates.** `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk]` exercises the production `Review` criterion. It asserts `source_node_id`, `criterion_path` and `version` directly. It then runs verification, storage, reuse and `check_inputs`, so the trait's methods are called through the SDK's own type.\n- **Exports.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the exported inventory matches the book.\n\nThe per-method obligations (`answer`, `source_node_id`, `version`, `execute`) are refined by sub-requirements that have their own tests. The universal \"every criterion type\" claim can't be tested exhaustively, and the implementation sites carry it. I found no plausible violation of the trait shape that these tests would accept. The only unmet {fixme}-style concern is none: there are no acknowledgments, and I found no gap.",
  "references": "56f4b9fe7da0aa15014b3838508c84537c402f91531e0b341d3e3ebb1b623825",
